From 8f081d676cd0d2d2bf9b3cfcbe12e6fcd7d5d727 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:48:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bluetooth: say why a device disconnected, and authorize off
 the loop

BlueZ gives the reason of each disconnection in the Disconnected signal
of the device (org.bluez.Reason.*), and in its own log only when it runs
with debugging. A remote that keeps dropping its connection left nothing
in the logs that tells a lost key from a radio link that timed out, a
remote that saves power, or the standby of this add-on. Log each
disconnection with its reason, the address (the filter of the
diagnostics and of "Submit log" turns it into a marker), the kind of
device and the maker's IDs; and log the disconnections that the standby
of this add-on asks for. The name of a device, which may be a person's,
is not logged.

When a paired device does not accept the key that is kept for it (the
kernel saw "PIN or Key Missing" when it started the encryption, which
BlueZ reports as org.bluez.Reason.Authentication), it was paired again
with another system since, or it lost its pairing. A device keeps one key
per computer, and Windows and LibreELEC on one PC are one computer to it:
on a PC that starts both, pairing in one breaks the other. BlueZ then
retries three times and stops connecting to it, so the device just looks
dead or flaky. Say what happened in the log and, once per device and
session, on the screen.

RequestAuthorization put its dialog up on the asyncio loop, so no other
D-Bus message was served while it was open, not even the Cancel that
BlueZ sends when it gives up waiting. Run it as the other agent methods
are run (run_dialog). Hades asks there before a device that is already
paired is paired again (JustWorksRepairing=confirm), which a classic
(BR/EDR) device may ask for after it was paired in Windows. (An LE device
cannot: while a key is kept for it, the kernel tries that key again.)
